{"metadata":{"citation_count_without_self_citations":203,"citation_count":227,"publication_info":[{"year":2019,"page_end":"214","page_start":"1","journal_issue":"1","journal_title":"Acta Mat.","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214888"},"journal_volume":"222"},{"year":2019,"material":"publication","page_end":"214","page_start":"1","journal_title":"Acta Math.","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214886"},"journal_volume":"222","pubinfo_freetext":"Acta Math., 222 (2019), 1-214"}],"dois":[{"value":"10.4310/acta.2019.v222.n1.a1","source":"International Press"},{"value":"10.4310/ACTA.2019.v222.n1.a1","source":"arXiv","material":"publication"}],"titles":[{"title":"The linear stability of the Schwarzschild solution to gravitational perturbations","source":"International Press"},{"title":"The linear stability of the Schwarzschild solution to gravitational perturbations","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"b71eb032-b460-439a-ae4c-7850e9048ea4","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1044876"},"full_name":"Dafermos, Mihalis","signature_block":"DAFARNm","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Mathematics, Princeton University, Princeton, New Jersey, U.S.A.; and Dept. of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, United Kingdom"}]},{"uuid":"3de255a8-ccd1-407a-9861-0493b70d6cb4","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1274922"},"full_name":"Holzegel, Gustav","signature_block":"HALSAGALg","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Mathematics, Imperial College London, United Kingdom"}]},{"uuid":"9f122b22-9dba-41e7-8c20-002c4f0ecaff","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1050881"},"full_name":"Rodnianski, Igor","signature_block":"RADNANSCi","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Mathematics, Princeton University, Princeton, New Jersey, U.S.A."}]}],"curated":false,"license":[{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint"},{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","imposing":"arXiv"}],"texkeys":["Dafermos:2016uzj"],"citeable":true,"imprints":[{"date":"2019"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"We prove in this paper the linear stability of the celebrated Schwarzschild family of black holes in general relativity: Solutions to the linearisation of the Einstein vacuum equations (“linearised gravity”) around a Schwarzschild metric arising from regular initial data remain globally bounded on the black hole exterior, and in fact decay to a linearised Kerr metric. We express the equations in a suitable double null gauge. To obtain decay, one must in fact add a residual pure gauge solution which we prove to be itself quantitatively controlled from initial data. Our result a fortiori includes decay statements for general solutions of the Teukolsky equation (satisfied by gauge-invariant null-decomposed curvature components). These latter statements are in fact deduced in the course of the proof by exploiting associated quantities shown to satisfy the Regge–Wheeler equation, for which appropriate decay can be obtained easily by adapting previous work on the linear scalar wave equation. The bounds on the rate of decay to linearised Kerr are inverse polynomial, suggesting that dispersion is sufficient to control the non-linearities of the Einstein equations in a potential future proof of non-linear stability. This paper is self-contained and includes a physical-space derivation of the equations of linearised gravity around Schwarzschild from the full non-linear Einstein vacuum equations expressed in a double null gauge.","source":"International Press"},{"value":"We prove in this paper the linear stability of the celebrated Schwarzschild family of black holes in general relativity: Solutions to the linearisation of the Einstein vacuum equations around a Schwarzschild metric arising from regular initial data remain globally bounded on the black hole exterior and in fact decay to a linearised Kerr metric. We express the equations in a suitable double null gauge. To obtain decay, one must in fact add a residual pure gauge solution which we prove to be itself quantitatively controlled from initial data. Our result a fortiori includes decay statements for general solutions of the Teukolsky equation (satisfied by gauge-invariant null-decomposed curvature components). These latter statements are in fact deduced in the course of the proof by exploiting associated quantities shown to satisfy the Regge--Wheeler equation, for which appropriate decay can be obtained easily by adapting previous work on the linear scalar wave equation. The bounds on the rate of decay to linearised Kerr are inverse polynomial, suggesting that dispersion is sufficient to control the non-linearities of the Einstein equations in a potential future proof of nonlinear stability. This paper is self-contained and includes a physical-space derivation of the equations of linearised gravity around Schwarzschild from the full non-linear Einstein vacuum equations expressed in a double null gauge.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"raw_refs":[{"value":"[1] Andersson, L. & Blue, P., Hidden symmetries and decay for the wave equation on the Kerr spacetime. Ann. of Math., 182 (2015), 787–853.","schema":"text","source":"desy"}],"reference":{"misc":["Hidden symmetries and decay for the wave equation on the Kerr spacetime"],"label":"1","texkey":"AndBlue","authors":[{"full_name":"Andersson, L."},{"full_name":"Blue, P."}],"publication_info":{"year":2015,"page_end":"853","page_start":"787","journal_title":"Annals Math.","journal_volume":"182"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1257974"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[2] — Uniform energy bound and asymptotics for the Maxwell field on a slowly rotating Kerr black hole exterior. J. Hyperbolic Differ. Equ., 12 (2015), 689–743.","schema":"text","source":"desy"}],"reference":{"misc":["— Uniform energy bound and asymptotics for the Maxwell field on a slowly rotating Kerr black hole exterior"],"label":"2","texkey":"andersson2013uniform","publication_info":{"year":2015,"page_end":"743","page_start":"689","journal_title":"J.Hyperbol.Diff.Equat.","journal_volume":"12"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1120268"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[3] Aretakis, S., Horizon instability of extremal black holes. Adv. Theor. Math. Phys., 19 (2015), 507–530.","schema":"text","source":"desy"}],"reference":{"misc":["Horizon instability of extremal black holes"],"label":"3","texkey":"Aretakis2","authors":[{"full_name":"Aretakis, S."}],"publication_info":{"year":2015,"page_end":"530","page_start":"507","journal_title":"Adv.Theor.Math.Phys.","journal_volume":"19"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/85511"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[4] Bardeen, J. M. & Press, W. H., Radiation fields in the Schwarzschild background. J. Math. Phys., 14 (1973), 7–19.","schema":"text","source":"desy"}],"reference":{"misc":["Radiation fields in the Schwarzschild background"],"label":"4","texkey":"bardeen1973","authors":[{"full_name":"Bardeen, J.M."},{"full_name":"Press, W.H."}],"publication_info":{"year":1973,"page_end":"19","page_start":"7","journal_title":"J.Math.Phys.","journal_volume":"14"}}},{"raw_refs":[{"value":"[5] Bieri, L. & Zipser, N., Extensions of the Stability Theorem of the Minkowski Space in General Relativity. AMS/IP Studies in Advanced Mathematics, 45. Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, RI, 2009.","schema":"text","source":"desy"}],"reference":{"misc":["Extensions of the Stability Theorem of the Minkowski Space in General Relativity. AMS/IP Studies in Advanced Mathematics, 45. Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, RI"],"label":"5","texkey":"Lydia","authors":[{"full_name":"Bieri, L."},{"full_name":"Zipser, N."}],"publication_info":{"year":2009}}},{"record":{"$ref":"http